+# Six-Week Personal Knowledge Map
+
+## Focus
+
+- Primary theme: Sleep consistency
+- Time horizon: Six weeks
+- Tags: `#sleep`, `#attention`, `#energy`
+- Scope: All six inbox notes remain in scope; digital distraction and energy are supporting threads.
+
+## Linked Evidence
+
+| Connection | Supporting notes | What the notes show | Caveat |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Stable day boundaries may support steadier energy | SLP-01; LGT-02 | Observed: across 14 nights, the eight nights aligned to 22:45–23:15 lights-out and a 07:00 wake had median next-day energy of 4/5; the six irregular nights had a median of 2/5. A 20-minute outdoor walk within one hour of waking occurred on 10 mornings: seven reached at least 4/5 alertness before 10:00; among five mornings without it, one did. Hypothesis: consistent evening and morning cues may complement each other. | These small, subjective, non-randomized observations do not establish causation; sleep duration, workload, caffeine, and weather were uncontrolled. |
+| Fewer phone cues may protect planned attention | NTF-03; FCS-04 | Observed: over five weekdays there was a daily median of 63 non-call alerts; 11 timed work interruptions had a median 18-minute resettling time. Of 12 planned 50-minute blocks at 09:30, seven of nine with the phone in another room were completed, versus one of three with it on the desk. Hypothesis: batching checks at 12:30 and 17:00, using Do Not Disturb, and moving the phone may help a block finish. | The audit cannot show every alert caused delay; the timed sample risks selection bias, the block groups were uneven, and block difficulty was not logged. |
+| Energy has several plausible, separable inputs | SLP-01; LGT-02; MOV-05 | Observed: on eight workdays with a 15-minute walk at 12:45, six had 15:30 energy of at least 4/5; among five workdays without the walk, one did. Hypothesis: sleep regularity can remain the anchor while morning light and midday movement provide context. | The comparison was small and non-randomized; lunch timing and morning workload were uncontrolled, so the walk cannot be credited as the cause. |
+| A review can join evidence without merging measures | RVW-06; SLP-01; FCS-04; MOV-05 | Observed: all six completed Sunday reviews preceded weeks with at least four of five priorities finished; one of three skipped reviews did. Hypothesis: a review can coordinate learning while preserving separate outcomes. | Week difficulty and existing capacity varied, so review may mark easier weeks rather than produce them. |
+
+## Evergreen Notes
+
+- **Consistency becomes useful when its boundary and outcome are explicit.** SLP-01 defines a sleep-window condition and next-day rating; RVW-06 supplies weekly aggregation. Count adherence, compare the median, and keep a short personal pattern distinct from proof of cause.
+- **Attention protection combines environment and timing.** NTF-03 supplies fixed check times and Do Not Disturb; FCS-04 supplies the protected block and phone-placement field. Log alerts, interruptions, and completion as distinct observations within one reusable routine.
+- **Supporting energy cues can inform the main theme without displacing it.** LGT-02 supplies morning context and MOV-05 an afternoon signal, while SLP-01 remains central. Compare those records without claiming that any one behavior produced a rating.
+- **Weekly synthesis should preserve uncertainty and measurement boundaries.** RVW-06 joins counts, a relevant median, one surprise, and one adjustment from SLP-01, FCS-04, and MOV-05 without collapsing three experiments into one score or causal story.
+
+## Experiments
+
+| Experiment | Cue | Action | Measure | Review |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Anchor the sleep window | At the nightly 22:45–23:15 window. | Put lights out inside that window, wake at 07:00, and record both times plus next-day energy on SLP-01’s 1–5 scale. | Count aligned nights and calculate median next-day energy, retaining workload and caffeine as caveats. | At Sunday 18:00, compare adherence and energy as observations, not proof of cause. |
+| Protect one focus block and batch notifications | At 09:30 on each planned workday. | Put the phone in another room, enable Do Not Disturb, complete one 50-minute focus block, and defer non-urgent checks to 12:30 and 17:00. | Record planned, started, and completed blocks, phone placement, alerts encountered, and interruptions without inventing a daily delay estimate. | At Sunday 18:00, compare completion and interruptions while retaining the uneven historical samples. |
+| Test the midday walk | At 12:45 on a workday. | Take the 15-minute walk from MOV-05. | Log whether it occurred and record energy at 15:30 on the same 1–5 scale; compare weekly counts while retaining workload and lunch as caveats. | At Sunday 18:00, review walk adherence and 15:30 ratings separately from sleep and focus outcomes. |
+
+## Review Rhythm
+
+- Meet the map every Sunday at 18:00, using RVW-06’s review pattern to close the week and prepare the next one.
+- Build a weekly scorecard with sleep-window nights and median next-day energy; focus blocks planned, started, and completed plus interruptions; and midday walks completed plus the 15:30 energy ratings.
+- Interpret changes as personal observations from small, subjective, non-randomized samples. Record a surprising result and competing explanations, and do not describe correlation as causation.
+- Keep the three experiments separately measurable before writing a short cross-note synthesis: sleep adherence does not substitute for focus completion, and neither substitutes for the walk and afternoon-energy record.
+
+## Source Index
+
